/* GuroPrep JHS Science (Grades 7–10). Loads after src/science/items-and-activities-g3-6-term3.js (SCIB, s_bank). Keys are grade-term-index. */
/* ===================== JHS Science Grade 10 ===================== */
Object.assign(SCIB,{
'10-1-0':{F:[["Which is an indicator of a chemical reaction?","formation of a gas","change in shape","melting","cutting"],["A solid that forms when two solutions mix is a ____.","precipitate","solvent","solute","vapor"],["Which shows a temperature change in a reaction?","a hand warmer getting hot","ice cubes in a drink","boiling water in a kettle","wet clothes drying"],["Which is a sign of a chemical reaction in food?","milk smells sour","rice is poured","water is boiled","bread is sliced"]],
 C:["List five indicators of a chemical reaction.|color change, precipitate forms, gas is released, odor is produced, temperature changes","Is the bubbling of boiling water a sign of a chemical reaction? Explain.|No; it is a change of state (physical), not a new substance.","What indicators do you observe when vinegar is added to baking soda?|bubbles (gas released) and a slight temperature drop"],
 E:["Why should you use more than one indicator to conclude that a chemical reaction happened?|Some indicators (like bubbles or color change) can also occur in physical changes.","Describe indicators observed when an iron nail rusts.|color change to reddish-brown, new flaky substance forms over time","Explain why a change in temperature can indicate a chemical reaction.|Breaking and forming bonds absorbs or releases energy."],
 act:'Do safe mini-reactions: vinegar + baking soda, milk + vinegar, steel wool in vinegar, and a dissolving effervescent tablet. Record the indicators observed for each.'},
'10-1-1':{F:[["Which is an acid?","hydrochloric acid","sodium hydroxide","saline solution","distilled water"],["Which is a base?","sodium hydroxide","vinegar","calamansi juice","hydrochloric acid"],["Saline solution is ____.","neutral (a salt solution)","strongly acidic","strongly basic","a gas"],["Phenolphthalein turns ____ in a base.","pink","colorless","yellow","blue"]],
 C:["What color does universal indicator show for a strong acid, neutral solution, and strong base?|red, green, purple (dark blue/violet)","How does red litmus paper behave in a base?|It turns blue.",["Which indicator stays colorless in an acid?","phenolphthalein","universal indicator","red cabbage indicator","methyl orange"],"What pH range is basic?|above 7 (up to 14)"],
 E:["Plan a safe procedure to identify three unknown colorless solutions as acid, base or neutral.|Use small samples with litmus paper and universal indicator; record colors and pH; wear goggles.","Why do different indicators change color at different pH values?|Each indicator is a different dye that changes structure at a certain pH.","Why should hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide be handled only with teacher supervision?|They are corrosive and can burn skin and eyes."],
 act:'Using litmus paper, phenolphthalein (if available), and a red cabbage indicator, test labeled solutions (diluted vinegar, soap, salt water, baking soda solution). Record colors and classify each.'},
'10-1-2':{F:[["A + B → AB is a ____ reaction.","combination (synthesis)","decomposition","single replacement","double replacement"],["AB → A + B is a ____ reaction.","decomposition","combination","single replacement","combustion"],["A + BC → AC + B is a ____ reaction.","single replacement","double replacement","decomposition","combination"],["AB + CD → AD + CB is a ____ reaction.","double replacement","single replacement","decomposition","combination"]],
 C:["Classify: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.|combination","Classify: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂.|decomposition","Classify: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂.|single replacement","Classify: AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl + NaNO₃.|double replacement"],
 E:["Give an everyday example of a decomposition reaction.|hydrogen peroxide breaking down into water and oxygen on a wound; baking soda decomposing when heated","Why does a single replacement reaction happen only with a more reactive element?|The more reactive element can take the place of the less reactive one.","Predict the products and type: Mg + CuSO₄ → ?|MgSO₄ + Cu; single replacement"],
 act:'Sort reaction cards (word and formula equations) into combination, decomposition, single replacement and double replacement. Then do one safe example of a reaction with your teacher.'},
'10-1-3':{F:[["Burning fuel with oxygen is ____.","combustion","photosynthesis","neutralization","rusting only"],["Acids react with carbonates to produce ____.","carbon dioxide","oxygen","nitrogen","hydrogen only"],["Acids react with many metals to produce ____ gas.","hydrogen","oxygen","carbon dioxide","nitrogen"],["Which reaction uses carbon dioxide and releases oxygen?","photosynthesis","combustion","respiration","rusting"]],
 C:["How does acid rain damage buildings and statues?|Acids react with carbonate stone (limestone, marble), releasing CO₂ and weakening the stone.","How does burning fossil fuels affect the environment?|It releases CO₂ (global warming) and pollutants like sulfur dioxide (acid rain).","Why are photosynthesis and respiration important to the natural environment?|They cycle oxygen and carbon dioxide and provide energy for living things."],
 E:["Using secondary sources, explain how one reaction impacts the built environment in the Philippines.|Answers vary; e.g., corrosion of metal roofs near the sea, combustion in vehicles causing smog.","Why are coral reefs harmed by more CO₂ in the ocean?|CO₂ makes seawater more acidic, dissolving carbonate needed for coral skeletons.","Explain how combustion can be both useful and harmful.|Useful: cooking, transport, power; harmful: pollution, fires, greenhouse gases."],
 act:'Research one type of reaction (combustion, acid on metals, acid on carbonates, photosynthesis, or respiration) and its effects on the natural and built environment. Make an infographic with sources.'},
'10-1-4':{F:[["In a chemical equation, the substances on the left are the ____.","reactants","products","catalysts","solvents"],["The substances on the right are the ____.","products","reactants","indicators","solutes"],["The arrow in an equation means ____.","yields (produces)","equals exactly","is mixed with","is heated"],["In a chemical equation, (aq) means ____.","dissolved in water","solid","gas","liquid only"]],
 C:["Write a word equation for the burning of methane.|methane + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water","Write the formula equation for hydrogen reacting with oxygen to form water (unbalanced).|H₂ + O₂ → H₂O","Why do scientists use chemical equations?|They show reactants, products, and amounts in a short, universal way."],
 E:["Write the word and formula equations for the reaction of zinc with hydrochloric acid.|zinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + hydrogen; Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂","What do the symbols (s), (l), (g), and (aq) tell readers?|the states: solid, liquid, gas, aqueous (dissolved)","Write the word equation for photosynthesis and its formula equation.|carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen; 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂"],
 act:'Write word and formula equations for five everyday reactions (burning methane, rusting, baking soda and vinegar, photosynthesis, respiration). Include state symbols.'},
'10-1-5':{F:[["The law of conservation of mass states that in a chemical reaction, mass is ____.","neither created nor destroyed","always lost","always gained","doubled"],["In a chemical reaction, atoms are ____.","rearranged","destroyed","created","changed into other elements"],["Who is known for the law of conservation of mass?","Antoine Lavoisier","Isaac Newton","Dmitri Mendeleev","Albert Einstein"]],
 C:["Why does a burning log seem to lose mass?|Gases (CO₂, water vapor) escape into the air; total mass of all products equals reactants.","If 10 g of reactants react completely in a closed container, what is the mass of the products?|10 g","Why should experiments on conservation of mass be done in a closed system?|so no gas escapes and all mass can be measured"],
 E:["A sealed bag with vinegar and baking soda has a mass of 150 g before mixing. What is the mass after the reaction (sealed)? Explain.|150 g; atoms are rearranged but none leave the sealed bag.","Explain why a balanced equation demonstrates conservation of mass.|The same number of each type of atom appears on both sides.","Why does a rusting nail gain mass?|Oxygen from the air combines with the iron."],
 act:'Put vinegar in a plastic bottle and baking soda in a balloon. Measure the total mass, then mix by lifting the balloon and measure again. Explain using conservation of mass.'},
'10-1-6':{F:[["To balance an equation, you change the ____.","coefficients","subscripts","elements","states"],["Balance: H₂ + O₂ → H₂O","2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O","H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O","H₂ + 2O₂ → H₂O","2H₂ + 2O₂ → H₂O"],["Balance: Mg + O₂ → MgO","2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO","Mg + O₂ → 2MgO","Mg + 2O₂ → MgO","2Mg + 2O₂ → MgO"],["Balance: Na + Cl₂ → NaCl","2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl","Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl","Na + 2Cl₂ → NaCl","2Na + 2Cl₂ → NaCl"]],
 C:["Balance: N₂ + H₂ → NH₃|N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃","Balance: CH₄ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O|CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O","Balance: Zn + HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂|Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂","Why can’t you change subscripts when balancing?|Changing subscripts changes the substance itself.","Balance: H₂O₂ → H₂O + O₂|2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂"],
 E:["Balance: Fe + O₂ → Fe₂O₃|4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃","Balance: C₃H₈ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O|C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O","Balance: Al + O₂ → Al₂O₃|4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃","Balance: C₆H₁₂O₆ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O|C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O"],
 act:'Use colored beads or paper circles to represent atoms. Build the reactants and products for H₂ + O₂ → H₂O and other equations, then balance them by adding whole molecules.'},
'10-1-7':{F:[["Which increases the rate of most chemical reactions?","higher temperature","lower temperature","bigger lumps","less concentration"],["Refrigerating food slows spoilage because ____.","lower temperature slows reactions","cold kills all bacteria","it adds preservatives","it removes water"],["Powdered sugar dissolves and reacts faster than sugar cubes because of greater ____.","surface area","mass","color","temperature"],["A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up is a ____.","catalyst","reactant","product","inhibitor"]],
 C:["Name four factors that affect reaction rate.|temperature, concentration, surface area (particle size), catalyst (also pressure for gases)","How does painting iron slow down corrosion?|It keeps oxygen and water from reaching the iron.","Why does blowing on a fire make it burn faster?|It supplies more oxygen (higher concentration of a reactant).",["Salting and drying fish slows spoilage mainly by ____.","removing water needed by bacteria","adding oxygen","increasing temperature","adding a catalyst"]],
 E:["Explain how a catalytic converter in cars reduces pollution.|It contains catalysts that speed up reactions converting harmful gases into less harmful ones.","Why are fire extinguishers effective? Relate to reaction factors.|They remove heat, oxygen, or fuel, stopping the combustion reaction.","Explain why fine dust (flour, coal dust) can explode in factories.|Huge surface area lets combustion happen extremely fast."],
 act:'Drop effervescent tablets (whole vs. crushed) into cold and warm water. Time how long each reaction takes. Relate your results to food preservation and fire control.'},
'10-1-8':{F:[["A reaction that releases heat is ____.","exothermic","endothermic","neutral","physical"],["A reaction that absorbs heat is ____.","endothermic","exothermic","combustion","explosion"],["Burning wood is ____.","exothermic","endothermic","neither","only physical"],["An instant cold pack works by an ____ process.","endothermic","exothermic","nuclear","electrical"]],
 C:["Is photosynthesis exothermic or endothermic? Explain.|endothermic; it absorbs light energy","Why does a mixture feel cold during an endothermic reaction?|It absorbs heat from its surroundings, including your hand.","Give an everyday exothermic reaction.|burning LPG, respiration, a hand warmer, cement setting"],
 E:["Explain exothermic and endothermic reactions in terms of bond breaking and forming.|Breaking bonds absorbs energy; forming bonds releases energy; the overall difference decides whether energy is released or absorbed.","Draw (describe) an energy diagram for an exothermic reaction.|products are lower in energy than reactants; energy released","Why is respiration considered exothermic?|It releases energy from glucose for the body."],
 act:'Measure temperature changes when mixing: water + baking soda + vinegar, water + calcium chloride (if available), and yeast + hydrogen peroxide. Classify each as exothermic or endothermic.'},
'10-1-9':{F:[["Homeostasis is the body’s ____.","maintenance of a stable internal balance","growth in height","digestion only","sleep cycle only"],["Normal human body temperature is about ____.","37°C","25°C","40°C","30°C"],["Which is an indicator of homeostasis?","blood glucose level","hair color","shoe size","height"],["Which organ helps control blood glucose?","pancreas","heart","lungs","stomach"]],
 C:["Name three indicators the body keeps in balance.|body temperature, blood glucose, blood pressure (also water balance, pH)","What happens when you exercise in hot weather to keep body temperature stable?|You sweat and blood vessels in the skin widen to release heat.","Why is homeostasis necessary for survival?|Cells and enzymes work only within narrow conditions."],
 E:["Explain what can happen if blood glucose is not controlled.|Too high (as in diabetes) can damage organs; too low can cause dizziness or fainting.","How does drinking water help maintain homeostasis?|It replaces water lost and keeps blood volume and temperature regulation working.","Why does a fever happen during infection, and why is a very high fever dangerous?|The body raises temperature to fight germs, but very high temperatures can damage proteins and organs."],
 act:'Measure your pulse and (if available) body temperature at rest, after 2 minutes of exercise, and after 5 minutes of rest. Explain the changes as homeostasis at work.'},
'10-1-10':{F:[["Negative feedback ____ a change to bring the body back to normal.","reverses","increases","ignores","copies"],["Shivering when cold is an example of ____ feedback.","negative","positive","no","random"],["Contractions during childbirth that get stronger are an example of ____ feedback.","positive","negative","no","balanced"]],
 C:["Explain how the body uses negative feedback to control blood glucose after eating.|Glucose rises → pancreas releases insulin → cells take in glucose → level returns to normal.","Why is positive feedback less common in the body?|It increases change, which can be dangerous unless it leads to a clear endpoint.","Give another example of positive feedback.|blood clotting"],
 E:["Draw (describe) a feedback loop for body temperature regulation.|Stimulus (temperature rises) → sensor (brain) → effectors (sweat glands, blood vessels) → response (cooling) → temperature returns to normal.","Explain how glucagon and insulin work together.|Insulin lowers high blood glucose; glucagon raises low blood glucose.","Why is a home thermostat a good model of negative feedback?|It turns cooling or heating on and off to keep a set temperature."],
 act:'Make a flow chart of two feedback loops: one negative (body temperature or blood glucose) and one positive (childbirth or blood clotting). Present and compare them.'},
'10-1-11':{F:[["Natural selection was proposed by ____.","Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace","Isaac Newton","Gregor Mendel only","Louis Pasteur"],["In natural selection, organisms with helpful traits are more likely to ____.","survive and reproduce","die early","stop reproducing","change their DNA on purpose"],["Natural selection acts on ____ within a population.","variation","identical copies","rocks","weather"]],
 C:["Describe the steps of natural selection.|variation → overproduction → competition → survival of the fittest → helpful traits passed on","Why do bacteria become resistant to antibiotics over time?|Resistant bacteria survive and reproduce, so the population changes.","Does an individual organism evolve during its life? Explain.|No; populations evolve over generations through changes in trait frequencies."],
 E:["Using secondary sources, explain the peppered moth example of natural selection.|During industrial pollution, dark moths survived better on soot-covered trees and became more common.","Explain how natural selection could change a beak shape in a bird population.|Birds with beaks suited to available food survive and reproduce more, so that beak shape becomes common.","Why is natural selection described as the primary mechanism of evolution?|It explains how populations become adapted to their environment over time."],
 act:'Play a natural selection game: hide different colored paper “insects” on a patterned background. “Predators” pick them up for 30 seconds. Count survivors over three rounds and explain.'},
'10-1-12':{F:[["Differences among individuals of the same species are called ____.","variation","isolation","extinction","mutation only"],["Passing traits from parents to offspring is ____.","heredity","selection","adaptation","isolation"],["A trait that helps an organism survive in its environment is an ____.","adaptation","isolation","extinction","disease"],["When populations are separated and cannot interbreed, this is ____.","isolation","selection","variation","heredity"]],
 C:["How can geographic isolation lead to new species?|Separated populations face different environments and change differently until they can no longer interbreed.","Give an example of an adaptation of a Philippine animal.|the tarsier’s large eyes for night vision; the mudskipper’s ability to breathe air","How do variation and selection work together?|Variation provides different traits; selection favors those that help survival."],
 E:["Discuss how island environments in the Philippines led to many unique species.|Isolation on islands allowed populations to evolve separately, producing many endemic species.","Explain the difference between an acquired trait and an inherited adaptation.|Acquired traits (like muscles from exercise) are not passed on; adaptations are inherited.","How do these concepts explain why Philippine cloud rats differ across islands?|Isolated populations adapted to different islands, leading to different species."],
 act:'In small groups, discuss one example (e.g., Philippine cloud rats or tarsiers). Explain how variation, heredity, isolation, selection and adaptation work in that example. Present a concept map.'},
'10-1-13':{F:[["Preserved remains of ancient organisms are ____.","fossils","cells","minerals only","rocks only"],["Similar bone structures in different animals (like a human arm and a bat wing) are ____.","homologous structures","vestigial structures","analogous only","fossils"],["The study of where organisms live around the world is ____.","biogeography","geology","astronomy","meteorology"]],
 C:["How do fossils support evolution?|They show changes in organisms over time and transitional forms.","How does comparative morphology support evolution?|Similar structures suggest common ancestry.","Give an example of a vestigial structure in humans.|the appendix, tailbone (coccyx), wisdom teeth"],
 E:["Explain how biogeography of island species supports evolution.|Island species resemble nearby mainland species but have unique traits, suggesting they evolved from mainland ancestors.","Using secondary sources, describe a transitional fossil.|e.g., Archaeopteryx (features of reptiles and birds); Tiktaalik (fish and tetrapod features)","Why is DNA comparison strong evidence for evolution?|Closely related species share more similar DNA sequences."],
 act:'Compare pictures of forelimb bones of a human, a bat, a whale and a cat. Color matching bones the same. Use your diagram and other evidence to explain support for evolution.'},
'10-2-0':{F:[["Carrying capacity is ____.","the largest population an environment can support","the number of species","the size of an animal","the speed of growth"],["Which factor limits carrying capacity?","food supply","the color of animals","the name of the place","the season name"],["Factors that limit population growth are called ____ factors.","limiting","growing","helping","neutral"]],
 C:["List four factors that limit carrying capacity.|food, water, shelter, mates (also disease, predators, space)","How can a drought affect the carrying capacity of a grassland?|Less water and food lowers the number of animals it can support.","Why can a population exceed carrying capacity for a short time?|Resources may still be available, but they will be used up and the population will then decline."],
 E:["Explain how carrying capacity applies to fish in a fishpond.|Food, oxygen and space limit how many fish can survive; overstocking leads to deaths.","Discuss how human activities can raise or lower carrying capacity.|Irrigation and food production can raise it; pollution and habitat destruction lower it.","Why is carrying capacity important in managing fisheries?|Catches must stay within what the population can replace."],
 act:'Simulate a population of “deer” and limited “food cards” for several rounds. Record population size each round and identify the carrying capacity on a graph.'},
'10-2-1':{F:[["As a population nears carrying capacity, its growth ____.","slows down","speeds up","stays the same","doubles"],["A graph of population growth that levels off has an ____ shape.","S","J","straight line","circle"],["Exponential growth (J-curve) happens when ____.","resources are unlimited","resources are very limited","there is no reproduction","all organisms die"]],
 C:["Why does population growth slow near carrying capacity?|Competition for food, water and space increases; birth rates fall and death rates rise.","Describe the stages of an S-shaped growth curve.|slow start, rapid growth, slowing growth, leveling off at carrying capacity","What happens if a population overshoots carrying capacity?|It usually declines as resources run short."],
 E:["Interpret: a rabbit population grows quickly, then stays around 500 for many years. What is the carrying capacity?|about 500","Why do invasive species often show rapid growth at first?|They have plenty of resources and few predators in the new area.","How can human population growth be related to carrying capacity?|Technology has raised Earth’s capacity, but resources are still limited."],
 act:'Grow yeast in sugar water and count bubbles (or use given data). Graph population over time and identify the exponential growth and carrying capacity parts of the curve.'},
'10-2-2':{F:[["Biotechnology is the use of ____ to make products or solve problems.","living things or their parts","only machines","rocks","only computers"],["Which is an example of biotechnology?","making yogurt with bacteria","building a house","painting a wall","melting ice"],["Biotechnology is used in ____.","medicine, agriculture and food","only sports","only art","only transport"]],
 C:["Give one example of biotechnology in medicine.|insulin made by genetically engineered bacteria; vaccines","Give one example of biotechnology in agriculture.|disease-resistant crops, tissue culture of bananas","What is the difference between traditional and modern biotechnology?|Traditional uses organisms as they are (fermentation); modern changes DNA or cells directly."],
 E:["Explain why biotechnology is important for the Philippines.|It can improve crops, food security, medicine and industries using local resources.","Give an example of biotechnology for the environment.|bacteria that clean oil spills (bioremediation)","Why must biotechnology be regulated?|to ensure safety for people and the environment and to address ethical concerns"],
 act:'Make a chart of biotechnology examples in your community or the news (food, medicine, farming, environment). Classify each as traditional or modern.'},
'10-2-3':{F:[["Nata de coco is made by fermenting ____.","coconut water","rice","fish","milk"],["Vinegar is made when bacteria turn alcohol into ____.","acetic acid","sugar","salt","protein"],["Soy sauce is made by fermenting ____.","soybeans (with wheat)","coconut","mango","rice only"],["Cheese is made from ____.","milk fermented by bacteria (and enzymes)","fish","coconut","fruit juice"]],
 C:["Which microorganism is used to make nata de coco?|acetic acid bacteria (Komagataeibacter/Acetobacter xylinus)","Give two other Filipino fermented products.|patis, bagoong, burong isda, tapuy, basi, puto (any two)","Why is fermentation considered traditional biotechnology?|It uses natural microorganisms without changing their genes."],
 E:["Explain the role of microorganisms in making vinegar from sugarcane or coconut sap.|Yeast first ferment sugar into alcohol; acetic acid bacteria then convert alcohol into acetic acid.","Why are fermented foods safer to store?|Acids, alcohol or salt produced inhibit harmful bacteria.","Using secondary sources, describe one Philippine fermented product and its process.|Answers vary; must cite source."],
 act:'Research (or with teacher help, observe) one traditional fermented product such as nata de coco, vinegar, or soy sauce. Make a flow chart of its production and the microorganisms involved.'},
'10-2-4':{F:[["A genetically modified organism (GMO) has ____.","DNA that has been changed by genetic engineering","no DNA","only natural breeding","no cells"],["Golden Rice was developed to provide ____.","vitamin A (beta-carotene)","more sugar","less water","more fat"],["Bt crops contain a gene that helps them ____.","resist certain insect pests","grow in snow","glow in the dark","need no sunlight"],["In vitro fertilization (IVF) is fertilization ____.","outside the body in a laboratory","inside the soil","using plants only","by wind"]],
 C:["Why was Golden Rice developed for countries like the Philippines?|to help reduce vitamin A deficiency","What is genetic engineering?|changing an organism’s DNA by adding, removing or changing genes","Give one example of modern biotechnology in medicine.|insulin from engineered bacteria, gene therapy, modern vaccines, IVF"],
 E:["Using secondary sources, describe how Bt eggplant or Bt corn works.|A gene from Bacillus thuringiensis makes a protein toxic to certain insect larvae, reducing pesticide use.","Compare traditional breeding and genetic engineering.|Traditional breeding mixes whole genomes over generations; genetic engineering changes specific genes directly.","Explain one benefit and one concern of GMOs.|Benefit: higher yield or nutrition; concern: ecological effects, labeling, or corporate control of seeds"],
 act:'Research one example of modern biotechnology (Golden Rice, Bt corn, IVF, insulin production). Make a fact sheet: how it works, benefits, and concerns. Cite your sources.'},
'10-2-5':{F:[["A debate presents ____.","arguments for and against an issue","only one opinion","a story","a song"],["An ethical issue asks whether something is ____.","right or wrong","fast or slow","big or small","hot or cold"],["Which is a societal issue about biotechnology?","whether GMO foods should be labeled","the color of a lab coat","the price of pencils","the shape of a microscope"]],
 C:["Give one environmental concern about GMOs.|cross-pollination with wild plants, effects on non-target insects, reduced biodiversity","Give one ethical issue about modern reproductive technologies.|what to do with unused embryos, cost and access, designer babies","Why should debates use evidence from reliable sources?|so arguments are accurate and convincing"],
 E:["Prepare two arguments for and two against using genetically modified crops in the Philippines.|Answers vary; must be balanced and supported by evidence.","Explain how a decision about biotechnology can involve science, ethics and economics.|Science shows effects; ethics asks what is right; economics considers costs, farmers and markets.","Write a short closing statement for one side of the debate.|Answers vary."],
 act:'Hold a class debate: “Should genetically modified crops be widely grown in the Philippines?” Prepare evidence from reliable sources and use a debate rubric.'},
'10-2-6':{F:[["Which is evidence of global warming?","rising average global temperatures","more snow everywhere","colder oceans","shrinking deserts only"],["Melting glaciers and ice sheets cause ____.","sea level rise","sea level fall","no change","colder air"],["Coral bleaching is linked to ____.","warmer ocean temperatures","colder water","more fish","less sunlight"]],
 C:["Give three pieces of evidence of climate change.|rising temperatures, melting ice, rising sea levels, stronger extreme weather, coral bleaching (any three)","How is climate different from weather?|Weather is short-term conditions; climate is long-term average patterns.","Who studies and reports global climate evidence?|scientists, including the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC); PAGASA in the Philippines"],
 E:["Interpret: average global temperature has risen by about 1.1–1.3°C since pre-industrial times. Why does this small number matter?|Small average changes cause big effects: more heat waves, melting ice, sea-level rise, stronger storms.","Why do scientists use data over many decades?|To see long-term trends rather than year-to-year variations.","Explain how sea-level rise affects Philippine coastal communities.|flooding, erosion, saltwater in farms and wells, loss of homes"],
 act:'Analyze a graph of global average temperature (or local PAGASA data) over 100 years. Describe the trend and list other evidence of climate change you find in reliable sources.'},
'10-2-7':{F:[["Which is a greenhouse gas?","carbon dioxide","oxygen","nitrogen","argon"],["Greenhouse gases trap ____ in the atmosphere.","heat","light only","sound","rain"],["Which human activity adds the most carbon dioxide?","burning fossil fuels","planting trees","using solar panels","walking"],["Methane is released by ____.","rice paddies, livestock and landfills","solar panels","wind turbines","ocean waves"]],
 C:["Explain the enhanced greenhouse effect.|Extra greenhouse gases from human activities trap more heat, raising global temperatures.","Name three greenhouse gases.|carbon dioxide, methane, nitrous oxide, water vapor, CFCs (any three)","Why is deforestation linked to climate change?|Trees absorb CO₂; cutting and burning them releases CO₂ and reduces absorption."],
 E:["Compare the natural greenhouse effect and the enhanced greenhouse effect.|Natural: keeps Earth warm enough for life; enhanced: extra gases from humans cause extra warming.","Why is methane important even though there is less of it than CO₂?|It traps much more heat per molecule over a short time.","Suggest two ways to reduce greenhouse gas emissions in the Philippines.|renewable energy, efficient transport, reforestation, better waste management"],
 act:'Model the greenhouse effect with two jars: one with air and one with extra CO₂ (from vinegar and baking soda), both in sunlight. Record temperatures every 5 minutes and explain.'},
'10-2-8':{F:[["El Niño is associated with ____ in the Philippines.","drier conditions and drought","more typhoons always","snow","earthquakes"],["La Niña is often associated with ____ in the Philippines.","more rainfall","drought","volcanic eruptions","cold winters"],["ENSO stands for ____.","El Niño Southern Oscillation","Equator North Sea Ocean","Energy and Nature System Organization","El Niño Sea Order"]],
 C:["What happens to Pacific Ocean temperatures during El Niño?|Waters in the central and eastern Pacific become warmer than normal.","How does El Niño affect farmers in the Philippines?|Less rain can cause drought, crop loss and water shortages.","Which agency monitors ENSO for the Philippines?|PAGASA"],
 E:["Explain how a change in ocean temperatures far away can affect Philippine weather.|Changes in sea temperatures shift winds and rainfall patterns across the Pacific.","Suggest two actions communities can take to prepare for El Niño.|save water, plant drought-tolerant crops, adjust planting schedules, prepare for fires","Why can La Niña increase flood risks?|It tends to bring more rain and can strengthen monsoon rains."],
 act:'Use PAGASA climate outlooks or news reports to compare an El Niño year and a La Niña year in the Philippines. Make a table of their effects on rainfall, farming and water supply.'},
'10-2-9':{F:[["Which is a local impact of climate change in the Philippines?","stronger typhoons and floods","less sunlight forever","no more rain","colder winters"],["Which action helps reduce global warming?","turning off unused lights and appliances","burning trash","leaving the fridge open","cutting trees"],["Planting trees helps because trees ____.","absorb carbon dioxide","produce carbon dioxide only","cause floods","heat the air"]],
 C:["List three individual actions to reduce your carbon footprint.|save electricity, walk or use public transport, reduce waste and plastic, plant trees, eat less meat (any three)","How can climate change affect Philippine food supply?|droughts, floods and heat reduce crop yields and fish catches","Why do small individual actions matter?|Many people acting together greatly reduce emissions."],
 E:["Identify two climate change impacts in your province and suggest one adaptation for each.|Answers vary; e.g., flooding → better drainage; heat → more trees and shade.","Compare mitigation and adaptation.|Mitigation reduces greenhouse gases; adaptation prepares for impacts.","Make a one-week plan to reduce your household’s energy use.|Answers vary."],
 act:'Do a household or school energy audit for one week. Identify climate impacts in your area and make an action plan with ways individuals can reduce global warming.'},
'10-2-10':{F:[["Which is a renewable energy source?","solar","coal","oil","natural gas"],["The Philippines is one of the world’s top producers of ____ energy.","geothermal","nuclear","coal","oil"],["Which renewable source uses moving air?","wind","geothermal","hydro","biomass"],["Which renewable source uses falling or flowing water?","hydroelectric","solar","geothermal","coal"]],
 C:["How can renewable energy reduce climate change?|It produces electricity with little or no greenhouse gas emissions.","Name a wind farm in the Philippines.|Bangui Wind Farm (Ilocos Norte)","Why is the Philippines well suited for solar energy?|It is tropical and receives strong sunlight most of the year."],
 E:["Explain how the Philippines could make better use of its natural resources for energy.|expand geothermal, hydro, solar, wind and ocean energy; improve grids and storage","Give one limitation of renewable energy and how it can be addressed.|Solar and wind vary with weather; use batteries, mixed sources and better grids.","Why can renewable energy also improve energy security?|It reduces dependence on imported fuels and price changes."],
 act:'Research the energy mix of the Philippines (coal, natural gas, geothermal, hydro, solar, wind). Make a pie chart and propose how to increase renewable energy in your region.'},
'10-3-0':{F:[["Which technology is used to measure plate movement precisely?","GPS (GNSS) stations","thermometers","rain gauges","compasses only"],["Plates move about as fast as ____.","fingernails grow (a few cm per year)","a car","a walking person","a jet"],["Satellites that measure ground movement use ____.","radar and GPS","microscopes","telescopes for stars only","sound"]],
 C:["How do GPS stations measure plate movement?|They record their precise positions over time; changes show how the ground moves.","What is InSAR?|a satellite radar method that maps tiny ground movements","Why are these measurements important for the Philippines?|They help identify faults that store strain and assess earthquake hazards."],
 E:["Explain how scientists can tell that the Philippine Sea Plate is moving toward the northwest.|GPS stations on and near the plate show their positions changing in that direction over years.","Why must plate motion be measured over many years?|Movement is only a few centimeters per year.","Compare older and modern methods of studying plate movement.|Older: geological evidence and fossils; modern: GPS, satellite radar, seismometers"],
 act:'Use given GPS station data (positions over 10 years) to compute how far and in what direction a station moved. Plot the movement on a map.'},
'10-3-1':{F:[["At a divergent boundary, new crust is ____.","formed","destroyed","folded only","frozen"],["At a convergent oceanic–continental boundary, the oceanic plate ____.","subducts (sinks)","rises","stays still","melts the continent"],["At a transform boundary, crust is ____.","neither created nor destroyed","created","destroyed","melted"]],
 C:["What structures form at mid-ocean ridges?|ridges, rift valleys, new oceanic crust, shallow earthquakes","What events occur at subduction zones?|deep earthquakes, tsunamis, volcanic arcs, trench formation","What events occur at transform boundaries?|shallow earthquakes along faults"],
 E:["Make a table of boundary types with structures, movements and events.|Divergent: ridge/rift, apart, shallow quakes and volcanism; convergent: trench/mountains/volcanoes, together, strong quakes; transform: faults, sideways, shallow quakes.","Why are the strongest earthquakes usually at subduction zones?|Huge areas of plates lock and store strain before slipping.","Explain why the Philippines experiences events of convergent and transform boundaries.|It has subduction zones on both sides and large strike-slip faults."],
 act:'Make a three-panel model or poster of the boundary types showing structures, plate movements, and typical events. Give one real example for each.'},
'10-3-2':{F:[["Which is a major ocean trench near the Philippines?","Philippine Trench","Atlantic Trench","Indian Trench","Arctic Trench"],["Mount Apo is the ____ mountain in the Philippines.","highest","lowest","only","newest"],["Which is a major fault in the Philippines?","Philippine Fault Zone","San Andreas Fault","Alpine Fault","North Anatolian Fault"],["Which is an active volcano in the Philippines?","Mayon","Kilimanjaro","Fuji","Etna"]],
 C:["Where are most volcanoes in the Philippines located relative to trenches?|in chains (arcs) parallel to and inland from the trenches","Why does the Philippine Fault Zone run through the country?|Oblique convergence of plates creates sideways motion along the fault.","What does the location of the Manila Trench explain?|volcanoes in western Luzon (e.g., Pinatubo) and earthquakes there"],
 E:["Using a map, interpret how mountains, faults, volcanoes and trenches are related to plate tectonics in the Philippines.|They form belts parallel to subduction zones and major faults, showing plate interactions.","Why is Mindanao affected by more than one trench?|It is near the Philippine Trench and the Cotabato Trench, among others.","Explain why the Philippines is called a mobile belt.|It is a zone squeezed and deformed between major plates."],
 act:'On a map of the Philippine Archipelago, mark major mountains, faults, volcanoes and trenches using reliable sources. Write an interpretation of the pattern using plate tectonics.'},
'10-3-3':{F:[["To predict a future position, you need the plate’s ____.","speed and direction","color","age only","name"],["If a plate moves 7 cm per year, in 100 years it moves ____.","700 cm (7 m)","70 cm","7 km","7 cm"],["50 million years is ____.","5 × 10⁷ years","5 × 10⁵ years","50,000 years","500 years"]],
 C:["If the Philippine Sea Plate moves about 7 cm per year, how far would it move in 50 million years?|7 cm × 50,000,000 = 350,000,000 cm = 3,500 km","Why is this prediction uncertain?|Plate speeds and directions can change, and parts of plates may break or subduct.","What does it mean that plate motion is “relative”?|Speeds are measured compared with another plate or a reference frame."],
 E:["Draw (describe) a prediction of the Philippine Archipelago’s position and shape in 50 million years, assuming steady northwest motion.|Answers vary; should show movement northwest by thousands of km and possible collision or deformation with Asia/Taiwan region.","Explain how subduction might change the shape of the archipelago over time.|Some crust may be consumed, islands may collide and form mountains, new volcanoes may form.","Why do scientists use models and computer simulations for such predictions?|They can test different speeds and directions over long periods."],
 act:'Using an assumed plate velocity (e.g., 7 cm/year northwest), calculate the distance moved in 50 million years. Draw the predicted position on a map and list the assumptions.'},
'10-3-4':{F:[["The asthenosphere is ____.","a soft, slowly flowing layer of the upper mantle","the hard outer crust","the liquid outer core","the atmosphere"],["Heat inside Earth causes ____ in the mantle.","convection currents","freezing","tides","weather"],["The force caused by a sinking slab pulling a plate is ____.","slab pull","ridge push","gravity of the Moon","wind drag"]],
 C:["Explain mantle convection.|Hot mantle rock rises, cools and sinks, creating slow currents that help move plates.","What is ridge push?|Newly formed, elevated crust at ridges slides away under gravity, pushing plates.","Why is slab pull considered a strong driving force?|Dense oceanic plates sinking into the mantle pull the rest of the plate."],
 E:["Using secondary sources, compare mantle convection, ridge push and slab pull.|Convection: heat-driven flow; ridge push: gravity at ridges; slab pull: weight of sinking plates (often strongest).","Why is the asthenosphere important for plate movement?|Its softness lets the rigid plates slide over it.","Explain the energy source for plate motion.|heat from Earth’s formation and radioactive decay inside Earth"],
 act:'Model mantle convection with warm water and food coloring (or a pot of miso soup/ginataan). Observe the currents and relate them to how plates move on the asthenosphere.'},
'10-3-5':{F:[["When an oceanic plate subducts, the plate above it often has ____.","volcanoes","no activity","only deserts","glaciers"],["A deep trench forms where ____.","one plate sinks under another","plates move apart","plates slide sideways","rivers meet the sea"],["Magma forms above subduction zones because ____.","water released from the sinking plate lowers the melting point of mantle rock","the core rises","the sea boils","the crust freezes"]],
 C:["List three effects of subduction on the overriding plate.|volcanic arcs, earthquakes, mountain building, uplift and deformation","Why are subduction zone earthquakes often very strong?|Large plate surfaces lock and release huge amounts of energy.","Why can subduction cause tsunamis?|Sudden vertical movement of the seafloor displaces water."],
 E:["Explain how subduction at the Manila Trench formed volcanoes like Pinatubo.|The South China Sea plate subducts; water released melts mantle rock, forming magma that rises.","Why is the overriding plate sometimes compressed into mountains?|Pushing plates squeeze and fold the crust.","Describe the sequence from subduction to volcanic eruption.|plate sinks → releases water → mantle melts → magma rises → collects → erupts"],
 act:'Use two sheets of foam or cardboard to model subduction. Mark where the trench, volcanoes and earthquakes would form on the overriding plate.'},
'10-3-6':{F:[["The Himalayas formed from a collision between ____.","the Indian and Eurasian plates","the Pacific and Nazca plates","Africa and South America","Australia and Antarctica"],["The Andes formed where ____.","the oceanic Nazca Plate subducts under South America","two oceans meet","the Atlantic opens","the Arctic freezes"],["Mount Everest is part of the ____.","Himalayas","Andes","Alps","Rockies"]],
 C:["Why are the Himalayas still growing?|India is still pushing into Asia.","Why are there many volcanoes in the Andes but few in the Himalayas?|The Andes form above a subducting oceanic plate; the Himalayas form from two continents colliding with little subduction.","How do marine fossils on high mountains support plate tectonics?|Rocks that formed under the sea were pushed up by plate collisions."],
 E:["Compare how the Himalayas and the Andes formed.|Himalayas: continent–continent collision, folding and thickening; Andes: oceanic–continental subduction, volcanism and uplift.","Explain why continent–continent collisions create the highest mountains.|Neither continent easily sinks, so crust crumples and thickens upward.","Why are earthquakes common in both mountain ranges?|Plates are still converging and building stress."],
 act:'Model mountain building by pushing layers of colored clay or towels together. Compare with a model of subduction. Explain how the Himalayas and Andes formed.'},
'10-3-7':{F:[["A projectile is an object that moves under the influence of ____ after being launched.","gravity only (ignoring air resistance)","a motor","wind only","magnetism"],["The path of a projectile is a ____.","parabola (curve)","straight line","circle","zigzag"],["Ignoring air resistance, the angle that gives the maximum range is ____.","45°","90°","10°","0°"],["A ball thrown straight up has ____ horizontal range.","zero","maximum","infinite","negative"]],
 C:["How does increasing release speed affect range?|It increases both range and maximum height.","Why does a basketball shot at a higher angle reach a higher height but may land shorter?|More of the velocity goes upward and less goes forward.","Which two angles give the same range (ignoring air resistance)?|complementary angles, e.g., 30° and 60°"],
 E:["Plan an investigation using a ball or paper rocket to test how release angle affects range.|Keep release speed and height the same; change angle (15°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 75°); measure range; repeat trials.","Why do real projectiles not always reach maximum range at 45°?|Air resistance and launch height change the best angle.","Explain the horizontal and vertical motion of a projectile.|Horizontal velocity stays constant (no air resistance); vertical velocity changes due to gravity."],
 act:'Shoot a ball or rubber band (safely) at different angles with the same force. Measure the range and maximum height. Graph range vs. angle.'},
'10-3-8':{F:[["In an elastic collision, objects ____.","bounce apart and kinetic energy is conserved","stick together","lose all energy","stop moving"],["In an inelastic collision, objects may ____.","stick together or deform, losing kinetic energy","bounce perfectly","gain energy","disappear"],["Which is closest to an elastic collision?","billiard balls hitting each other","a car crash","clay hitting a wall","a meteor hitting the ground"]],
 C:["Give two examples of inelastic collisions.|car crash, a ball of clay hitting the floor, a tackle in football (any two)","Is momentum conserved in both elastic and inelastic collisions?|Yes (in a closed system).","Where does kinetic energy go in an inelastic collision?|into heat, sound and deformation"],
 E:["Classify: a basketball bouncing on the floor (it bounces lower each time). Explain.|partly inelastic; some kinetic energy becomes heat and sound","Why are cars designed to have inelastic collisions?|Crumpling absorbs energy and protects passengers.","Compare elastic and inelastic collisions in terms of momentum and kinetic energy.|Both conserve momentum; only elastic conserves kinetic energy."],
 act:'Collide marbles with marbles (nearly elastic) and clay balls with clay balls (inelastic) on a track. Record what happens after each collision and classify.'},
'10-3-9':{gen:['collision'],F:[["When two objects collide, they exert ____ forces on each other.","equal and opposite","unequal","no","only upward"],["In a collision where objects stick together, the collision is ____.","perfectly inelastic","perfectly elastic","not a collision","explosive"]],
 C:["Using a model with two carts, what happens to a moving cart’s speed after it sticks to a stationary cart of equal mass?|It moves at half its original speed (momentum conserved)."],
 E:["Explain the forces and their effects when a moving cart hits a stationary one in your model.|Each cart pushes the other with equal and opposite force; the moving cart slows and the other speeds up."],
 act:'Use toy carts or marbles to model collisions. Add clay or Velcro to make them stick (inelastic) or let them bounce (elastic). Measure speeds before and after and describe the forces.'},
'10-3-10':{gen:['momentum'],F:[["Momentum depends on ____.","mass and velocity","color and shape","temperature","height only"],["The unit of momentum is ____.","kg·m/s","newton","joule","watt"],["A truck and a bicycle move at the same speed. Which has more momentum?","the truck","the bicycle","both are equal","neither has momentum"]],
 C:["Why does a bullet have large momentum even though its mass is small?|Its velocity is very high."],
 E:["Explain how momentum helps predict the impact of an object.|An object with more momentum needs more force or time to stop and causes greater impact."],
 act:'Roll balls of different masses at different speeds into a stack of paper cups. Record how many cups fall and relate the result to momentum.'},
'10-3-11':{gen:['momentum'],F:[["The more momentum an object has, the ____ it is to stop.","harder","easier","faster","same"],["Which is harder to stop?","a loaded jeepney at 40 km/h","a bicycle at 40 km/h","a ball at rest","a feather"]],
 C:["Why do trucks need longer distances to stop than cars at the same speed?|They have more mass, so more momentum."],
 E:["Design a guided investigation to show that more momentum makes an object harder to stop.|Roll carts with different masses or speeds into a padded barrier; measure stopping distance or how far the barrier moves."],
 act:'Roll a cart down a ramp from different heights and with different loads into a box filled with crumpled paper. Measure how far the box moves. Relate to momentum.'},
'10-3-12':{gen:['impulse'],F:[["To change an object’s momentum, a ____ must act over a period of time.","force","color","temperature","sound"],["Impulse equals force × ____.","time","mass","distance","speed"],["Catching an egg by moving your hands backward ____.","increases the stopping time and reduces the force","increases the force","stops it instantly","breaks the egg"]],
 C:["Why do athletes bend their knees when landing?|It increases the time to stop, reducing the force on their bodies."],
 E:["Explain why boxers move their heads back when punched.|It increases the time of impact, reducing the force."],
 act:'Drop eggs (in plastic bags) onto a hard surface and onto a pillow from the same height. Explain the results using force, time and change in momentum.'},
'10-3-13':{F:[["Seatbelts help by ____.","stopping passengers gradually and holding them in place","making cars faster","increasing impact force","replacing brakes"],["Airbags reduce injury by ____.","increasing the time it takes the head to stop","making the car heavier","speeding up the car","removing momentum instantly"],["Crumple zones in cars are designed to ____.","absorb energy and lengthen the collision time","make the car look nice","make the car stronger in the middle only","increase the force on passengers"]],
 C:["Explain how seatbelts reduce injury using momentum.|They spread the force over time and over the strong parts of the body as momentum changes.","Why are helmets padded inside?|The padding increases the time to stop the head, reducing force.","Why are highway barriers sometimes filled with water or sand?|They absorb energy and increase collision time."],
 E:["Using secondary sources, describe one vehicle safety feature and the physics behind it.|Answers vary; must explain impulse and momentum.","Why should children ride in proper car seats?|They hold children safely and spread collision forces over the body.","Design (describe) a device to protect an egg in a 2-m drop and explain the physics.|Answers vary; should increase stopping time and spread force."],
 act:'Do an egg-drop challenge: design a container that protects an egg dropped from 2 m. Explain how your design reduces the force of impact (like crumple zones and airbags).'},
'10-3-14':{gen:['collision'],F:[["The law of conservation of momentum states that in a closed system, total momentum ____.","stays the same before and after a collision","always increases","always decreases","becomes zero"],["When a person jumps off a small boat, the boat moves backward because of ____.","conservation of momentum","gravity only","friction only","magnetism"]],
 C:["A gun recoils when fired. Explain using conservation of momentum.|Before firing, total momentum is zero; the bullet gains forward momentum, so the gun gains equal backward momentum."],
 E:["Why is momentum conserved but kinetic energy not always conserved?|Forces between colliding objects are equal and opposite, so momentum is conserved; energy can change into heat or sound."],
 act:'Use two carts with a compressed spring between them (or two people on rolling chairs pushing apart). Measure the distances they move and explain using conservation of momentum.'},
'10-3-15':{F:[["A device that changes voltage is a ____.","transformer","battery","bulb","switch"],["Electricity is sent over long distances at ____ voltage to reduce energy loss.","high","low","zero","changing"],["A substation (grid station) ____.","steps voltage down for local distribution","generates all electricity","stores water","measures rainfall"],["The device that measures electricity use in a home is the ____.","electric meter","thermometer","ammeter only in schools","barometer"]],
 C:["Trace electricity from the power plant to your home.|power plant → step-up transformer → high-voltage transmission lines → substation (step-down) → distribution lines → pole transformer → electric meter → home","Why does high-voltage transmission reduce energy loss?|At high voltage, less current is needed for the same power, so less energy is lost as heat in wires.","What unit does an electric meter use to measure energy?|kilowatt-hour (kWh)"],
 E:["Explain why high-voltage lines are dangerous and how the public is protected.|They carry very high voltages; lines are placed high, insulated, fenced, and marked with warnings.","Compute: a 1,000-W appliance runs 3 hours a day for 30 days. How many kWh?|1 kW × 3 h × 30 = 90 kWh","Why do distribution companies use transformers near neighborhoods?|to step down voltage to safe levels for homes (230 V in the Philippines)"],
 act:'Draw a flow diagram of how electricity travels from a power plant to your home. Read your household electric meter (or bill) and compute monthly energy use in kWh.'},
'10-3-16':{F:[["Which is a safe practice with household appliances?","unplug by holding the plug, not the cord","use appliances with wet hands","overload extension cords","use damaged cords"],["Before repairing an appliance, you should ____.","unplug it","leave it plugged in","pour water on it","touch the wires"],["Which device protects a home circuit from overloads?","circuit breaker","light bulb","remote control","doorbell"]],
 C:["Why is it unsafe to use an extension cord for many high-power appliances?|The total current may overheat the cord and cause a fire.","Why do some appliances have a third prong (ground)?|It safely directs leaking current to the ground, reducing shock risk.","What should you do if an appliance smells of burning plastic?|Turn it off and unplug it safely, and have it checked."],
 E:["Explain why water and electricity together are dangerous in bathrooms and kitchens.|Water lowers resistance, so current can pass through the body more easily.","Make five safety rules for using electrical devices at home.|Answers vary.","Why should you never put metal objects into outlets or toasters?|Metal conducts electricity and can cause electric shock."],
 act:'Do a home electrical safety check (without opening any device): look for frayed cords, overloaded outlets, and appliances near water. Make a safety checklist and share recommendations.'},
'10-3-17':{F:[["An electric motor changes ____ energy into ____ energy.","electrical; mechanical","mechanical; electrical","heat; light","chemical; sound"],["A generator changes ____ energy into ____ energy.","mechanical; electrical","electrical; mechanical","light; heat","sound; chemical"],["Both motors and generators use ____.","magnets and coils of wire","only batteries","only light","water only"]],
 C:["How are motors and generators similar?|Both use magnets, coils and the interaction between magnetism and electricity.","How are they different?|A motor uses current to produce motion; a generator uses motion to produce current.","Give one household example of a motor.|electric fan, washing machine, blender, water pump"],
 E:["Explain how turning a coil in a magnetic field produces electricity.|Moving the coil changes the magnetic field through it, inducing a current (electromagnetic induction).","Why can a small motor sometimes act as a generator?|If you spin its shaft, the coil moves in the magnetic field and produces a current.","Give one example of a generator in the Philippines and its energy source.|turbines in hydro, geothermal, wind or coal plants; diesel generators on islands"],
 act:'Build a simple motor (battery, magnet, coil of wire) or spin a small DC motor connected to an LED to make it act as a generator. Compare the two.'},
'10-3-18':{F:[["Which helps reduce electricity use at home?","turning off lights when not in use","leaving appliances on standby all day","opening the fridge often","using old incandescent bulbs"],["LED bulbs use ____ energy than incandescent bulbs.","less","more","the same","no"],["Which appliance usually uses the most electricity in Filipino homes?","air conditioner","phone charger","LED bulb","radio"]],
 C:["Give three ways to reduce electrical energy use at home.|use LED bulbs, unplug unused appliances, use fans instead of aircon when possible, set aircon to 24–25°C (any three)","What local benefits come from saving electricity?|lower bills, fewer brownouts, less pollution","What global benefits come from saving electricity?|fewer greenhouse gas emissions and slower climate change"],
 E:["Compute savings: replacing a 60-W bulb with a 9-W LED used 5 hours a day for 30 days.|saves 51 W × 150 h = 7,650 Wh = 7.65 kWh per month","Propose a community project to reduce electricity use in your barangay.|Answers vary; must be practical.","Explain why energy efficiency is important for an archipelago that imports much of its fuel.|It reduces fuel imports, costs, and emissions."],
 act:'In a class discussion, list ways to reduce electricity use at home and school. Compute possible monthly savings for one change and explain the local and global benefits.'},
'10-3-19':{F:[["Which is a nonrenewable energy source used in the Philippines?","coal","solar","wind","geothermal"],["Burning coal releases ____.","carbon dioxide and pollutants","only oxygen","nothing harmful","pure water"],["Which renewable source does not burn fuel?","solar","coal","natural gas","diesel"]],
 C:["Give one advantage and one disadvantage of coal power.|Advantage: stable, large supply of power; disadvantage: pollution and greenhouse gases","Give one advantage and one disadvantage of hydroelectric power.|Advantage: renewable, no fuel; disadvantage: affects rivers, displaces communities, depends on rain","Why does the Philippines still rely on nonrenewable sources?|They provide steady baseload power and existing plants are in use; renewable capacity is still growing."],
 E:["Using secondary sources, evaluate how one renewable and one nonrenewable power source in the Philippines affect people and the environment.|Answers vary; must cite sources and give balanced effects.","Explain the impact of energy choices on human health.|Burning fuels causes air pollution and illness; cleaner energy reduces these risks.","Propose a balanced energy plan for your region.|Answers vary; must consider reliability, cost, environment."],
 act:'Research the electricity sources in your region. Make a table comparing renewable and nonrenewable sources by cost, reliability, and effects on people and the environment. Cite your sources.'}
});
